  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Justify that the reaction 2Na(s) + H₂(g) → 2NaH(s) is an oxidation-reduction reaction. (3 marks)', 'Assign oxidation numbers. In the reactants Na and H₂ are elements, oxidation number 0. In NaH (sodium hydride, an ionic compound Na⁺H⁻) Na is +1 and H is −1. Sodium''s oxidation number increases from 0 to +1 - it loses an electron and is oxidised (Na → Na⁺ + e⁻), acting as the reducing agent. Hydrogen''s decreases from 0 to −1 - it gains an electron and is reduced (H₂ + 2e⁻ → 2H⁻), acting as the oxidising agent. Since oxidation and reduction occur together, the reaction is an oxidation-reduction (redox) reaction.', null, '[{"point": "Oxidation numbers: Na 0 → +1 (in NaH); H 0 → −1 (hydride)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Na loses electrons - oxidised (reducing agent); H gains electrons - reduced (oxidising agent); both occur together → redox", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
