  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Explain the first and second laws of thermodynamics with an everyday example. (3 marks)', 'First law: energy can neither be created nor destroyed, only converted. Heat Q supplied to a system goes partly to raise its internal energy and partly into work: Q = ΔU + W. Example: in a car engine the chemical energy of fuel becomes heat, part of which does work moving the car and the rest raises the temperature of the engine and exhaust - the total energy is conserved.
Second law: heat flows naturally from a hotter body to a colder one, never the reverse by itself, and no engine can convert all the heat it takes in into work; the entropy (disorder) of an isolated system always increases. Example: a cup of hot tea always cools to room temperature, never heats up by itself; a refrigerator can move heat from inside to the warmer room only because electrical work is supplied.', null, '[{"point": "First law: energy is conserved - heat supplied = increase in internal energy + work done (Q = ΔU + W); example: heat from fuel in a pressure cooker/ car engine converted to internal energy and work", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Second law: heat flows spontaneously from hot to cold only; no heat engine converts all heat into work (entropy of an isolated system increases); example: hot tea cools in a room, a refrigerator needs work to move heat out", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'engineering-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
    324, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
