  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Explain briefly how electricity is generated in a nuclear fission power plant. (3 marks)', 'In the reactor core, fuel rods of uranium-235 undergo fission: a nucleus absorbs a slow neutron, splits into two smaller nuclei and releases a large amount of energy as heat plus 2-3 neutrons, which cause further fissions - a chain reaction. A moderator (water or graphite) slows the neutrons, and control rods (boron/ cadmium) absorb extra neutrons to keep the reaction steady.
The heat is removed by a coolant (water under pressure) and used in a heat exchanger/ boiler to turn water into high-pressure steam. The steam spins a turbine connected to a generator, which produces electricity by electromagnetic induction. The used steam is condensed back to water in a condenser (cooled by a cooling tower or river) and pumped back - the same steam cycle as a thermal plant, with nuclear fission as the heat source.', null, '[{"point": "Fission: U-235 nuclei absorb slow neutrons and split, releasing large energy (heat) and 2-3 neutrons → controlled chain reaction (moderator slows neutrons, control rods absorb excess)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Heat is carried by the coolant to a heat exchanger/ boiler to produce steam; steam drives a turbine coupled to a generator (electromagnetic induction); steam is condensed and recycled", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
